window.__hvBdRegister("phil-apollonius", {"key":"phil-apollonius","sections":[{"i":1,"j":62,"loc":"Book I 1–11","title":"Preface, birth, and youth at Aegae","summary":"Philostratus opens with Pythagoras and Empedocles as models of Apollonius' way of wisdom and rejects the charge that Apollonius was a wizard. He names his sources: the journal of Damis of Nineveh, which the empress Julia asked him to rework, Maximus of Aegae, Apollonius' letters and will, while dismissing Moeragenes. Apollonius is born at Tyana after his mother sees Proteus in a vision and swans sing at his birth. Schooled at Tarsus and then Aegae, he takes up the Pythagorean life at sixteen, gives up meat, wine and animal products, and lives in the temple of Asclepius. There he advises a drunken dropsical youth, exposes a rich Cilician who had seduced his stepdaughter, and teaches that the gods give each worshipper what he deserves.","ideas":["Foreknowledge through wisdom is distinguished from wizardry","Damis' journal is the main source, recast by Philostratus","Pythagorean regimen: no flesh, no wine, linen, long hair","Lavish sacrifice cannot buy the gods' favor"],"terms":[{"t":"magos (wizard)","d":"The charge of practicing illegitimate magic that Philostratus sets out to refute."},{"t":"Asclepius at Aegae","d":"The healing god whose temple Apollonius makes his home and who sends patients to him."}]},{"i":63,"j":117,"loc":"Book I 12–19","title":"Five years' silence and the meeting with Damis","summary":"Apollonius rebuffs a lustful governor of Cilicia and foretells his execution. On his father's death he shares his inheritance with his dissolute brother, reforms him, and gives most of the rest to poor relatives; he vows never to marry. He keeps silence for five years in Pamphylia and Cilicia, yet stops riots by gesture and at Aspendus forces grain-hoarders, by a written warning, to feed a starving city. At Antioch he criticizes the uncultured temple of Daphne, sets out his daily routine of rites and teaching, and describes his oracular, law-giver's style of speech. He resolves to visit the Magi and the Brahmans, leaves his timid followers, and at Nineveh gains Damis, who claims to know the languages of the East and records everything he can.","ideas":["Silence as a discipline of self-mastery","The Aspendus famine: the earth as mother of all","The sage speaks like a law-giver, not a questioner","Damis as admiring companion and record-keeper"],"terms":[{"t":"echemythia (Pythagorean silence)","d":"The five-year vow of silence Apollonius keeps, communicating only by looks and gestures."}]},{"i":118,"j":188,"loc":"Book I 20–29","title":"Through Mesopotamia to the gates of Babylon","summary":"At Zeugma Apollonius tells a customs officer he carries virtues, not slaves. He learns the language of animals among the Arabs, faces down a frightened satrap at the Babylonian frontier, and reads a slain lioness with eight cubs as a sign that he will stay with the king a year and eight months. A dream of stranded fish leads him to the Eretrians deported by Darius to Cissia, whose tombs he restores. Philostratus describes Babylon, its walls, the tunnel under the Euphrates and the palace halls, and notes that Damis was excluded from Apollonius' meetings with the Magi. Refusing to kiss the king's image, Apollonius tells the officials he brings the king virtues, and Vardanes takes his arrival as the fulfillment of a dream.","ideas":["Omens read by comparing wholes with wholes","Care for the exiled Eretrians, living and dead","Babylon's splendor described from Damis' report","The Magi are wise, but not in all respects"],"terms":[{"t":"Ears of the King","d":"Persian officials who receive reports and question Apollonius at Babylon."},{"t":"Vardanes","d":"The Parthian king who has recently recovered his throne and becomes Apollonius' host."}]},{"i":189,"j":257,"loc":"Book I 30–40","title":"Apollonius at the court of Vardanes","summary":"Apollonius walks through the palace without admiring it and declines to join the king's horse sacrifice, offering frankincense to the Sun instead. He explains his Pythagorean rule, refuses royal lodging and, of ten gifts offered, asks only that the Eretrians keep their land. When Damis urges him to take money, he answers that greed is the one fault no one forgives a sage, who lives under the eyes of virtue everywhere. He predicts and then sees a eunuch caught in love, and persuades the king to let him live as a harsher punishment. His sayings to the king follow, on rule, wealth and war, until after a year and eight months he leaves for India, asking rewards only for his host and the Magi.","ideas":["Bloodless worship versus animal sacrifice","A wise man finds Hellas everywhere","Chastity means resisting desire, not lacking it","Wealth is chaff; a king should spend it"],"terms":[{"t":"Nisaean horse","d":"The prized breed the king sacrifices to the Sun, a sacrifice Apollonius will not attend."}]},{"i":258,"j":308,"loc":"Book II 1–10","title":"Over the Caucasus to Nysa","summary":"Mounted on the king's camels, the travelers cross toward the Caucasus, which Philostratus describes along with the chained pard and the legend of Prometheus. Apollonius drives off an empusa with abuse. On the summit he tells Damis that height brings no knowledge of the divine unless the pure soul rises of itself. When Damis offers him date wine, he argues that it is still wine and declines, though he lets his companions drink and eat meat. They reach Mount Nysa and its shrine of Dionysus, and Philostratus discusses the conflicting Greek and Indian accounts of the god and praises Alexander for not climbing the mountain.","ideas":["The soul, not a mountaintop, approaches heaven","A substitute indulgence is no real abstinence","Rival Greek and Indian stories of Dionysus","Alexander kept his army sober by bypassing Nysa"],"terms":[{"t":"empusa (hobgoblin)","d":"A shape-shifting phantom that flees when Apollonius and his party revile it."}]},{"i":309,"j":371,"loc":"Book II 11–19","title":"Elephants and the crossing of the Indus","summary":"Watching a boy drive an elephant, Apollonius argues with Damis that the animal's own docility, not the rider, governs it. Philostratus records the elephant Ajax dedicated by Alexander, and argues against Juba that tusks are teeth, not horns. A herd crossing the Indus with the smallest in front prompts a long exchange on the natural love of animals for their young and on the tactical sense of the elephants' order. Vardanes' letter to the satrap of the Indus secures boats and a guide. The section ends with an account of the Indus, its flooding compared with the Nile's, and the king's sacrifice to the river.","ideas":["The elephant obeys by its own nature","Love of offspring is natural in all animals","The elephants' crossing order is wise, not foolish","The Indus floods without snow, unlike claims about the Nile"],"terms":[]},{"i":372,"j":433,"loc":"Book II 20–30","title":"Taxila, Porus, and the philosopher-king Phraotes","summary":"At Taxila the travelers see bronze reliefs of Porus and Alexander, and Philostratus relates Porus' noble sayings. While waiting, Apollonius leads Damis to agree that painting is imitation, that the mimetic faculty is natural and the painter's skill is art, and that viewers too must imitate in the mind. King Phraotes lives simply, pays off border tribes instead of fighting, eats no meat and reveals that he speaks Greek. Damis describes the banquet and its archery feats. Phraotes mocks false Greek philosophers and explains how Indian candidates for philosophy are tested by family record, character and features before crossing the Hyphasis.","ideas":["Painting as imitation; imitation as a faculty of mind","Porus preferred Alexander's admiration to his pity","Phraotes as a philosopher on the throne","Strict scrutiny of those admitted to philosophy"],"terms":[{"t":"mimēsis (imitation)","d":"The faculty, natural to humans, by which both painter and viewer form likenesses."},{"t":"Phraotes","d":"The young king of Taxila, trained by the Indian sages, who hosts Apollonius."}]},{"i":434,"j":472,"loc":"Book II 31–37","title":"Phraotes' story and the sleep of water-drinkers","summary":"Phraotes tells how his father, exiled as a boy, was trained by the sages and how he himself was raised by them and later recovered his kingdom. He says the true sages live beyond the Hyphasis and drove off Heracles and Dionysus with thunderbolts. At dawn the king and Apollonius debate whether wine-drinkers or water-drinkers sleep better. Apollonius argues that wine and drugs disturb the mind, that the sober soul sleeps calmly, and that it receives true dreams and oracles, citing the fasting rules of Amphiaraus.","ideas":["The sages beyond the Hyphasis are protected by the gods","Sobriety gives sound sleep and clear dreams","Oracles favor souls unclouded by wine","A king's philosophy should be moderate, not severe"],"terms":[{"t":"Oxydracae","d":"A warlike people met by Alexander, whom Phraotes distinguishes from the genuine sages."},{"t":"Amphiaraus","d":"The seer whose dream-oracle in Attica requires abstinence from wine."}]},{"i":473,"j":501,"loc":"Book II 38–43","title":"The treasure case and departure from Taxila","summary":"Phraotes postpones judgment because of the omens, then asks advice on a dispute over a treasure found in sold land. Apollonius says the gods would give the land and its treasure only to the better man, and an inquiry shows the buyer to be pious and the seller impious. The king replaces the worn camels, provides a guide and gifts, and writes to Iarchas, eldest of the sages, commending Apollonius. On the road the travelers see the trophy gates of Porus and Alexander, cross the Hydraotes, and reach Alexander's altars and column at the Hyphasis.","ideas":["Divine justice decides between litigants by their lives","Apollonius accepts only linen robes and a stone","The Hyphasis marks the limit of Alexander's advance"],"terms":[{"t":"Iarchas","d":"The oldest of the Indian sages, to whom Phraotes' letter is addressed."}]},{"i":502,"j":540,"loc":"Book III 1–15","title":"Wonders of India and the sages' hill","summary":"Philostratus describes the Hyphasis and its marvels: a nuptial balm, peacock fish, a worm yielding unquenchable fire, and the horned wild ass whose cup is said to protect against disease, which Apollonius doubts. The party sees a piebald woman sacred to Aphrodite, the pepper trees harvested by apes, the fertile Ganges plain, and the ways Indians hunt marsh, plain and mountain dragons. Near the sages' castle their guide is terrified, and a black youth with a gold anchor greets Apollonius by name. The hill, its cloud, its wells of testing and of pardon, its jars of rain and wind and its Greek statues are described, and Damis explains Apollonius' saying that the Brahmans live on earth and not on it.","ideas":["Marvels reported, some doubted by Apollonius","Dragon-hunting by charmed scarlet cloaks","The sages foreknow Apollonius' arrival","Levitation, sun-fire and self-supplying wealth"],"terms":[{"t":"Brahmans","d":"The Indian sages on the hill, who own nothing yet have all they need."},{"t":"well of testing and fire of pardon","d":"Sacred features of the hill used for oaths and for purification from involuntary sins."}]},{"i":541,"j":597,"loc":"Book III 16–25","title":"Iarchas on self-knowledge and former lives","summary":"Iarchas greets Apollonius, notes a missing letter in Phraotes' letter, and recounts his whole history. After a bath and a levitating hymn, Apollonius sits on Phraotes' throne and asks questions. Iarchas says the sages know all because they first know themselves, and call themselves gods because they are good. He claims to have been King Ganges, a founder of cities whom he ranks above Achilles, and points to a youth who was Palamedes and so hates philosophy. Iarchas tells Apollonius he was once an Egyptian pilot; Apollonius recounts outwitting pirates, but Iarchas replies that not doing wrong is not yet justice, and defends Tantalus as a good man.","ideas":["Self-knowledge is the ground of all knowledge","Reincarnation: Ganges, Palamedes, the Egyptian pilot","Abstaining from injustice is not the whole of justice","The poets wrong Tantalus and honor Minos"],"terms":[{"t":"metempsychōsis (transmigration)","d":"The Pythagorean and Indian teaching that souls pass into new bodies, illustrated by Iarchas' former lives."}]},{"i":598,"j":668,"loc":"Book III 26–37","title":"The vain king and the universe as animal","summary":"A local king arrives in pomp; automatic tripods and bronze cup-bearers serve the feast. The king sneers at Phraotes and the Greeks, but Apollonius, with Iarchas as interpreter, shows from the king's own words that Xerxes fled like a bad slave and that the Greeks honor even their defeated foes, and the king weeps and is reconciled to the Greeks. Iarchas explains why the sages are eighteen and criticizes choosing Olympic judges by lot. With Damis admitted, Iarchas teaches that the cosmos has five elements including ether, is a living creature of both sexes, and is ordered like a great Egyptian ship under God, with lesser gods as officers.","ideas":["Egyptian slanders had turned the king against the Greeks","Merit, not a fixed number or the lot, should choose","Ether is the stuff that divine natures breathe","The universe as an animal and as a ship under God"],"terms":[{"t":"aithēr (ether)","d":"The fifth element, of which the gods are made."},{"t":"cup of Tantalus","d":"The self-filling cup of friendship from which the sages and guests drink."}]},{"i":669,"j":712,"loc":"Book III 38–58","title":"Healings, divination, wonders, and the voyage home","summary":"The sages cure a boy possessed by a lovesick ghost by sending it a threatening letter, heal the lame, blind and paralyzed, and prescribe remedies for difficult births and an inherited weakness for wine. Apollonius shares their discussions of astrology, foreknowledge and sacrifice, receives seven planetary rings, and hears Iarchas praise divination as the source of medicine; Damis jokes about his own humble foresight. Iarchas dismisses the martichoras and shadow-footed men but affirms the pantarbe stone, griffins and the phoenix. After four months Apollonius leaves, sails down to the sea, describes the coasts, pearls and peoples, and returns by the Euphrates and Babylon to Cyprus and Ionia.","ideas":["Exorcism and healing by the sages","Purity of soul is required for true prophecy","Neither believing nor disbelieving every marvel","The return voyage along the Erythraean Sea"],"terms":[{"t":"pantarbe","d":"A glowing stone, obtainable only by rites, that draws other stones to itself."},{"t":"Peri thysiōn (On Sacrifices)","d":"Apollonius' treatise on pleasing sacrifice, which Philostratus says he found in several cities."}]},{"i":713,"j":740,"loc":"Book IV 1–10","title":"Ionia: Ephesus, Smyrna and the plague demon","summary":"Apollonius arrives in Ephesus to crowds, oracles in Ionia speak in his favour, and cities send deputations asking his counsel. He urges the Ephesians away from pantomimes and idleness, and uses the sparrows that fly off to share spilt barley as a lesson in sharing goods in common. At Smyrna he tells the citizens to take pride in good men rather than buildings, and teaches that a city needs rivalry for the common good mixed with concord, using a ship's crew as his image. When the plague comes to Ephesus he is summoned, has the people stone a blinking old beggar in the theatre, and under the stones a huge mad-looking hound is found where the beggar lay.","ideas":["Sparrows sharing found barley shame human selfishness","Good men travel and glorify their city more than its porticos","Healthy cities blend concord with rivalry in public service","The plague is ended by stoning a demon disguised as a beggar"],"terms":[{"t":"Averting god (Heracles)","d":"The protective Heracles whose statue stood in the Ephesian theatre and was set up over the spot where the plague-demon was killed."}]},{"i":741,"j":778,"loc":"Book IV 11–16","title":"Troy, the ghost of Achilles, Palamedes","summary":"After Pergamum Apollonius visits Ilium, spends a night alone on the mound of Achilles, and at dawn sends away Antisthenes of Paros because Achilles objects to a descendant of Priam. Sailing past Lesbos he finds and re-erects the buried statue of Palamedes near Methymna and visits the shrine where Orpheus' head once prophesied. Pressed by Damis, he recounts the meeting: Achilles rose from the barrow growing to twelve cubits, asked him to warn the Thessalians about neglected offerings, and answered five questions. Achilles said he and Patroclus share one urn, Polyxena killed herself for love of him, Helen was really in Egypt, and Homer left out Palamedes to spare Odysseus' shame.","ideas":["Apollonius summons Achilles by an Indian prayer, not blood-offerings","Achilles' five answers revise the Trojan story","Palamedes is honoured as the wise hero wronged by Odysseus","Apollonius is entrusted with an embassy to the Thessalians"],"terms":[{"t":"Palamedes","d":"Hero said to have invented writing and wisdom, unjustly killed at Troy through Odysseus, and omitted by Homer according to Achilles."}]},{"i":779,"j":805,"loc":"Book IV 17–24","title":"Athens, Eleusis, and the tour of Greece","summary":"At Athens the young philosophers throng him, but the hierophant refuses to initiate him as a wizard; Apollonius retorts and foretells who will initiate him four years later. He lectures on sacrifice and libations, drives a demon out of a dissolute Corcyrean youth, who knocks down a statue as a sign and then turns philosopher. He rebukes the effeminate dancing at the Dionysia and, by letter, the gladiatorial shows beneath the Acropolis. He goes as Achilles' envoy to the Thessalians, honours Leonidas and Megistias at Thermopylae, reforms rites at the Greek oracles, and predicts that the Isthmus will be cut and not cut, fulfilled by Nero's abandoned canal.","ideas":["The Eleusinian hierophant rejects him as impure; he predicts the successor","Exorcism of a possessed youth, marked by a falling statue","Attacks on soft Dionysiac dances and gladiatorial bloodshed","The Isthmus prophecy and Nero's failed canal"],"terms":[{"t":"hierophant","d":"The chief priest who revealed the sacred objects and admitted initiates at the Eleusinian mysteries."},{"t":"daimōn (demon)","d":"Spirit that the text says possessed the youth and spoke through him until Apollonius expelled it."}]},{"i":806,"j":869,"loc":"Book IV 25–33","title":"Corinth's lamia, Olympia and Sparta","summary":"In Corinth the Cynic Demetrius becomes his follower, and Apollonius exposes the rich Phoenician bride of the young Menippus as a lamia whose gold and servants vanish, and who confesses she was fattening him to eat. He has Bassus, a reputed parricide, silenced. At Olympia he reforms the effeminate Spartan envoys by letter, explains the statue of Milo as a priest of Hera, and scolds a young writer of encomia, adding that the supreme God should receive no sacrifice but only silent thought. In Sparta he answers questions on reverence, talks a seafaring descendant of Callicratidas back to a life on land, and advises a measured reply to Nero's angry letter.","ideas":["The lamia's wedding feast proves to be mere semblance","The First God, One and separate, is honoured by intellect, not offerings","Sparta is recalled to the ways of Lycurgus","Writing serves also to teach what not to write"],"terms":[{"t":"lamia (empusa)","d":"Female spirit that seduces young men with pleasures in order to devour their flesh; the translation also calls it a vampire."},{"t":"Demetrius the Cynic","d":"Corinthian philosopher of Cynic vigour who attaches himself to Apollonius and later defies Nero."}]},{"i":870,"j":919,"loc":"Book IV 34–40","title":"Crete and the road to Nero's Rome","summary":"A dream of Zeus' nurse sends Apollonius to Crete, where at the Asclepius shrine of Lebena he calms the crowd during an earthquake, and a new island is later found to have risen near Thera. Under Nero philosophy is suspected as magic and Musonius is imprisoned. Near Aricia the frightened Philolaus urges him to avoid Rome, and his band shrinks from thirty-four to eight; Apollonius praises those left and calls the tyrant a wilder beast than any lion. In Rome he dismisses a hired singer of Nero's songs, and the consul Telesinus, impressed by his prayer that the gods give him what is due, lets him live in the temples.","ideas":["Philolaus' fear becomes a touchstone of true disciples","Nero as a matricide and a beast fiercer for being stroked","Apollonius prays only that the gods grant what is due","He lodges among the temples, as the gods themselves travel"],"terms":[{"t":"Telesinus","d":"Roman consul inclined to religion who questions Apollonius and lets him reside in the temples."}]},{"i":920,"j":960,"loc":"Book IV 41–47","title":"Tigellinus' blank indictment and the raised girl","summary":"Demetrius denounces Nero's new baths and is banished by Tigellinus, who then has Apollonius watched. A thunderbolt that splits the cup at Nero's lips fulfils Apollonius' saying at an eclipse that something great would happen and not happen. Arrested for impiety over remarks on Nero's sore throat, he is saved when the accuser's scroll is found blank, and in private Tigellinus releases him as one no one can bind. Apollonius revives a bride of consular family on her bier, though Philostratus leaves open whether she was truly dead; he exchanges brief letters with the imprisoned Musonius, and when Nero bans philosophy in Rome he leaves for Gadeira.","ideas":["The charge scroll turns out to be blank","Apollonius ascribes his foresight to God, not prophecy","Raising of the dead girl, with an open question about her death","Musonius chooses to defend himself, unlike Socrates"],"terms":[{"t":"Tigellinus","d":"Nero's praetorian prefect, holding power of life and death, who prosecutes and then releases Apollonius."},{"t":"Musonius","d":"Philosopher imprisoned by Nero, called second only to Apollonius, who corresponds with him through Damis and Menippus."}]},{"i":961,"j":1003,"loc":"Book V 1–13","title":"Gadeira, Nero's stage tour, Vindex","summary":"Philostratus describes the Pillars of Heracles, the ocean tides (which Apollonius explained by spirits beneath the earth), and Gadeira with its altars to old age and death and its temple of the two Heracles. At Gadeira Apollonius mocks Nero's singing contests in Greece and the postponed Olympics, and news arrives of Nero's victories, which the locals misunderstand. After a secret talk with the governor of Baetica he says, “do not forget Vindex,” preparing the western revolt. In Sicily he calls the coming emperors “Thebans,” and Philostratus insists his foreknowledge came from the gods, not wizardry; he reads a three-headed baby at Syracuse as Galba, Vitellius and Otho.","ideas":["Tides explained as the breathing of a spirit under the earth","Nero's acting disgraces the imperial office","Apollonius quietly backs Vindex's rising","Foreknowledge from the gods is distinguished from magic"],"terms":[{"t":"Gadeira","d":"Cadiz, at the western edge of Europe, whose very religious people keep a temple to the Egyptian and Theban Heracles."},{"t":"goēs (wizard)","d":"One who claims to change fate by spirits, sacrifices or spells; Philostratus denies Apollonius was one."}]},{"i":1004,"j":1069,"loc":"Book V 14–23","title":"Etna, Aesop's fables, Greece and Rhodes","summary":"At Catana Apollonius argues that Aesop's fables teach better than poets' myths, because they claim no truth and end with a moral, and tells how Hermes, having handed out every other gift, gave Aesop the fable. He rejects the stories of Typhon or Hephaestus under Etna and explains volcanoes by asphalt, sulphur and wind underground. Back in Greece he avoids a ship that later sinks, is initiated at Eleusis, and hears from Demetrius how Musonius dug at the Isthmus in chains. He rebukes a dealer shipping images of the gods for sale, and in Rhodes questions the piper Canus about his art, a rich man about his house, and a boastful glutton.","ideas":["Fable is honest falsehood serving a useful moral","Giant and god myths about Etna are rejected for a natural account","Trading in divine images is impious","A house can own its owner"],"terms":[{"t":"muthologia (fable-making)","d":"Hermes' gift to Aesop: stories known to be false that carry a moral."}]},{"i":1070,"j":1115,"loc":"Book V 24–31","title":"Alexandria and Vespasian's appeal","summary":"Alexandria welcomes Apollonius as a god; he saves the falsely accused Pharion from execution by slowing the executioners until a messenger arrives. He objects to blood sacrifice by burning a model bull in incense and rebukes the Alexandrians for killing one another over horse races. Vespasian, coming to Egypt with an eye on the throne, seeks him out and asks to be made king; Apollonius says he already prayed for such a ruler, judges that Nero strung his lyre too slack and too tight, and prays to Capitoline Zeus, foreseeing the burning of the Capitol. Vespasian justifies his bid against the drunkard Vitellius, and Apollonius asks that Dion and Euphrates join their talks.","ideas":["Pharion is spared by Apollonius' foresight","Bloodless offering contrasted with Egyptian animal sacrifice","Vespasian seeks divine sanction through Apollonius","The burning of the Capitol is seen from Egypt"],"terms":[{"t":"Euphrates","d":"Stoic philosopher, at first an adviser alongside Apollonius, later his jealous enemy."},{"t":"Dion","d":"Dion of Prusa, a fluent orator and Vespasian's acquaintance, present at the debate."}]},{"i":1116,"j":1160,"loc":"Book V 32–37","title":"The debate on monarchy before Vespasian","summary":"Vespasian reviews the failures of the emperors since Tiberius and asks how to restore the throne. Euphrates, jealous, argues that he should have turned on Nero rather than the Jews, and urges him to restore a free popular government; Dion partly agrees, preferring that the Romans choose their own constitution. Apollonius replies that it is idle to make a ruler already acclaimed renounce power, that his sons and safety depend on it, and that one man ruling for the common good is popular government. He then gives Vespasian maxims of kingship: wealth to relieve the poor, fear of his own power, obedience to law, discipline of his sons, and governors who know their provinces' language. Euphrates' parting jibe at philosophy that claims commerce with the gods only harms himself.","ideas":["Euphrates and Dion argue for restoring democracy","Apollonius: monarchy of the best man can be popular rule","Kingship cannot be taught, but practical rules can be given","The debate begins the open rift with Euphrates"],"terms":[{"t":"basileia (kingship)","d":"Rule by one man; Apollonius calls it the greatest human attainment and one that cannot be taught."},{"t":"dēmokratia (popular government)","d":"The free constitution Euphrates and Dion recommend restoring to Rome."}]},{"i":1161,"j":1195,"loc":"Book V 37–43","title":"Gifts, quarrel with Euphrates, the lion Amasis","summary":"Vespasian loves Apollonius' stories of India, but Apollonius declines to sail with him, wishing to visit the Naked Sages and the Nile's sources. Offered gifts, he turns the emperor to the others: Dion asks reconciliation and a friend's release from the army, while Euphrates hands over a written list of requests, exposing his greed. The two then quarrel openly. Philostratus explains why Apollonius never met Vespasian again: three curt letters protest the removal of Greek liberty that Nero had granted. A tame lion is revealed as the soul of King Amasis and sent to Leontopolis, and Apollonius sets out up the Nile with ten companions, leaving Menippus to watch Euphrates.","ideas":["Euphrates' written petition belies his praise of democracy","Letters rebuking Vespasian for enslaving Hellas","A lion carries the reincarnated soul of Amasis","Only the trained follow him into Ethiopia"],"terms":[{"t":"Amasis","d":"Former king of Egypt whose soul, Apollonius says, lives in the tame lion."}]},{"i":1196,"j":1232,"loc":"Book VI 1–5","title":"Into Ethiopia: Timasion, Memnon, the exile","summary":"Philostratus compares Ethiopia with India: their rivers, beasts and peoples. At the frontier market of Sycaminus, where goods are left unguarded for barter, Apollonius praises trade without profiteering. He takes on the Egyptian youth Timasion, who fled his stepmother's advances, and praises him as more truly chaste than Hippolytus because he honours Aphrodite. They see the statue of Memnon speak at sunrise, and Apollonius purifies an unwilling killer the Naked Sages had refused, revealing that his victim descended from Thamus, who once ravaged their land.","ideas":["Silent barter as a model of honest exchange","Chastity that still reveres Aphrodite outranks Hippolytus'","The singing statue of Memnon at dawn","Apollonius purifies a homicide by Pythagorean rites"],"terms":[{"t":"katharsis (purification)","d":"Rites, prescribed by Empedocles and Pythagoras, that cleanse a man of blood-guilt."}]},{"i":1233,"j":1263,"loc":"Book VI 6–10","title":"The Naked Sages and Thespesion's challenge","summary":"The Naked Sages live on a hill near the Nile, worship the river, and keep a small portico for guests. Euphrates has sent Thrasybulus of Naucratis ahead to slander Apollonius as an arrogant wonder-worker, so the sages keep him waiting; Timasion uncovers the plot to Damis. Next morning the eldest, Thespesion, contrasts Indian display with Egyptian naked simplicity, makes an elm greet Apollonius to show they could work wonders too, and cites the plain answers of Delphic Apollo. He ends with Prodicus' Choice of Heracles, urging Apollonius to choose bare toil over the showy wisdom of India.","ideas":["Euphrates' agent poisons the sages against Apollonius","Thespesion's talking tree as a rebuke to Indian marvels","Truth needs no miracle-mongering","Prodicus' Heracles at the crossroads applied to two wisdoms"],"terms":[{"t":"Gymnoi (Naked Sages)","d":"Ascetic Ethiopian philosophers near the Nile, said to fall short of the Indians in wisdom."},{"t":"Choice of Heracles","d":"Prodicus' fable of Heracles choosing between Vice and Virtue, used by Thespesion."}]},{"i":1264,"j":1308,"loc":"Book VI 11–13","title":"Apollonius defends Indian wisdom","summary":"Apollonius replies that he chose his life long ago: Pythagorean wisdom, appearing among many alluring philosophies, offered him hardship and promised temperance, justice, fearlessness before tyrants and foreknowledge. His teacher persuaded him to go to the Indians, whom he likens to Aeschylus perfecting tragedy, and he says the Ethiopian sages were once Indians themselves and now malign their origins. He answers that Delphi is itself adorned with temples, offerings and verse oracles, that all art aims at ornament, and that even nakedness is a display. Thespesion blushes, Nilus crosses over to Apollonius, and Apollonius blames the hostility on Euphrates' slanders and the guilt of believing them.","ideas":["Wisdom personified offers the Pythagorean life of hardship","Ethiopian sages as Indian colonists who disowned their past","Ornament belongs to Delphic Apollo and to all art","Credulous listeners share the guilt of slander"],"terms":[{"t":"Pythagorean discipline","d":"Abstinence from meat, wine and wool, with silence; the way of life Apollonius says he chose."}]},{"i":1309,"j":1347,"loc":"Book VI 14–19","title":"Nilus joins; images of the gods","summary":"Apollonius tells the sages of his travels from Babylon onward, and the young Nilus dines with him and asks to become his disciple, explaining that he had hoped to find Indian wisdom among the sages and found only abuse of India. Apollonius accepts on condition that he not pester the sages with advice. Next day Apollonius asks why Egypt depicts its gods as animals. He argues that Phidias and Praxiteles worked by imagination, which can conceive what it has not seen, and not by mere imitation, and that animal images degrade the gods; Thespesion defends them as symbols and invokes Socrates swearing by the dog, which Apollonius explains as avoiding oaths by the gods.","ideas":["Nilus leaves the Naked Sages for Apollonius","Imagination outranks imitation in making divine images","Animal-headed gods invite contempt rather than awe","Imageless worship could leave the mind to picture the gods"],"terms":[{"t":"phantasia (imagination)","d":"The faculty that, Apollonius says, shaped the great Greek statues by conceiving what the eye has not seen."},{"t":"mimēsis (imitation)","d":"Copying what has been seen, which Apollonius ranks below imagination."}]},{"i":1348,"j":1388,"loc":"Book VI 20–22","title":"Spartan scourging and the just man","summary":"Thespesion challenges the Spartan scourging at the altar of Artemis; Apollonius defends it as a softened form of the Scythian blood offering, while Thespesion criticizes Spartan exclusion of strangers and asks him not to pry into ancient rites. On justice, Apollonius reports his past-life deceit of pirates as a ship's captain, which the Indians laughed at. Thespesion argues from Aristides' fair assessment of the allies' tribute that the just man is not the one who merely does no wrong, but who acts justly and keeps others from injustice, and Apollonius agrees. After talk of the soul's immortality along the lines of Plato's Timaeus, Apollonius prepares to visit the Nile's springs with Timasion as guide, while the sages speak privately to Nilus.","ideas":["Spartan whipping as a bloodless substitute for human sacrifice","Ancient rites should be respected, says Thespesion","Justice is more than abstaining from injustice","Aristides as the model of positive justice"],"terms":[{"t":"dikaiosunē (justice)","d":"Defined by Thespesion as doing just acts and preventing injustice in others, not merely avoiding wrong."}]},{"i":1389,"j":1407,"loc":"Book VI 23–27","title":"The Nile cataracts and the satyr","summary":"Leaving the sages, the party travels up the Nile past wild beasts, nomads and elephant-hunters, and Philostratus lists the far peoples of Ethiopia. They hear the cataracts; Damis turns back deafened at the second, while Apollonius, Timasion and Nilus reach the third, beyond which the springs are said to be impassable and haunted by spirits. In a village a satyr's ghost has been pursuing the women and killed two. Following the story of Midas, Apollonius pours wine into a trough, the unseen satyr drinks it and is found asleep in the cave of the nymphs, harmless from then on; Philostratus defends belief in satyrs.","ideas":["The three cataracts and the inaccessible springs","A satyr subdued with wine as Midas did","Philostratus vouches for satyrs from his own experience"],"terms":[{"t":"Catadupi","d":"The cataracts of the Nile, whose roar can deafen those who come too close."}]},{"i":1408,"j":1451,"loc":"Book VI 28–35","title":"Titus at Tarsus","summary":"After Ethiopia the feud with Euphrates widens. When Titus refuses a crown for taking Jerusalem, saying God did the work, Apollonius sends him a letter crowning him for moderation. Titus later meets him at Tarsus, fears ruling so young, and is told that obeying his father will make him like him; Apollonius quotes Archytas and gives him Demetrius the Cynic as a frank adviser. In private he warns Titus to fear his father's enemies and then his own kin, and foretells death “from the sea,” which Damis connects with the sea-hare Domitian used to poison him. He shames Titus into granting the Tarsians' petition at once, and Philostratus turns to selected later deeds.","ideas":["Titus crowned by letter for refusing a blood-won crown","Demetrius given to Titus as his barking 'dog'","Prophecy of Titus' death by his own kin","A sage must always remain himself"],"terms":[{"t":"sōphrosunē (temperance)","d":"Self-restraint, the crown Apollonius awards Titus for refusing honour for bloodshed."}]},{"i":1452,"j":1484,"loc":"Book VI 36–43","title":"Miscellaneous deeds in the cities","summary":"Apollonius tells a rich, ill-educated bird-trainer to stop spoiling birds' songs and learn rhetoric to fend off parasites. At Sardis he accepts the gold of the Pactolus but rejects the claim that trees are older than earth, and in Syria he uses an earthquake to reconcile feuding citizens. He buys an olive farm for a poor father of four daughters, who finds a hidden treasure and a rich harvest there, and cures a man of his passion for the Cnidian Aphrodite. He drives out Egyptian and Chaldaean frauds from the earthquake-struck Hellespont, mocks Domitian's edict against vineyards, and at Tarsus cures a boy bitten by a mad dog, saying the soul of Telephus is in him. Philostratus closes the book by summing up these deeds for cities and temples.","ideas":["Practical rebukes and help for individuals","Treasure found for the poor father via a land purchase","Like loves like: no marriage between god and mortal","The mad dog made healer of the wound it caused"],"terms":[{"t":"Telephus","d":"Mysian hero wounded and healed by the same spear; his soul, Apollonius says, is in the bitten boy."}]},{"i":1485,"j":1513,"loc":"Book VII 1–7","title":"Philosophers against tyrants; Apollonius defies Domitian","summary":"Philostratus opens Book VII by holding that conduct under despotism is the truest test of a philosopher, and lists famous stands against tyrants: Zeno of Elea, Plato with Dion, Phyton of Rhegium, the slayers of Cotys, Callisthenes, Diogenes and Crates. He then criticizes each as falling short of Apollonius, who faced a master of land and sea. Domitian is portrayed as vigorous, cruel and distrustful, with philosophers fleeing or flattering him. Apollonius instead rallies young senators and governors, and publicly mocks the tyrant in Asia: at a performance of the Ino, over the execution of three Vestals, and at Domitian's marriage to his niece Julia.","ideas":["Conduct under tyranny as the touchstone of a philosopher's character","Earlier philosophers' stands judged lesser than Apollonius' because of the scale of Domitian's power","Domitian's cruelty drives philosophy into hiding or flattery","Apollonius speaks openly against the tyrant in Ephesus and the provinces"],"terms":[{"t":"tyrannis (despotism)","d":"One-man rule held without law, the condition under which Philostratus tests the courage of philosophers."}]},{"i":1514,"j":1549,"loc":"Book VII 8–12","title":"The Smyrna oracle, the summons, and Demetrius' warning","summary":"Apollonius had corresponded with Nerva, Orfitus and Rufus, urging them against Domitian; when they were banished he spoke at Smyrna on the Fates, telling Domitian's statue that even a slain successor would live again. Euphrates reported this, and Domitian summoned Apollonius to use him as a pretext for condemning the consulars. Foreseeing the arrest, Apollonius sailed with Damis via Corinth to Dicaearchia, where he met Demetrius the Cynic. At Cicero's villa Demetrius explains the charges, chiefly that Apollonius sacrificed a boy for Nerva, and urges him to escape by ship rather than face a trial that cannot be fair.","ideas":["The Smyrna speech that destiny cannot be forced by tyrants","Domitian plans to convict the consulars through Apollonius","The charge of sacrificing a boy to read the future","Demetrius argues that dying on a false charge is no philosopher's death"],"terms":[{"t":"Moirai (Fates)","d":"The powers of destiny which, Apollonius argues at Smyrna, no tyrant can overrule."}]},{"i":1550,"j":1577,"loc":"Book VII 13–15","title":"Apollonius chooses to face trial for friends","summary":"Damis seconds Demetrius, pleading that Apollonius' death would be a defeat for philosophy and endanger his followers. Apollonius replies that law and nature require dying for freedom and kin, but the wise must die above all for their chosen tenets. He will not betray the accused consulars by fleeing; he imagines the shame of facing Thespesion, Phraotes and Iarchas as a traitor, and argues that conscience follows a man wherever he goes, praising or tormenting him. Persuaded, Demetrius blesses the enterprise; Damis resolves to go on, and at Apollonius' bidding sheds his Pythagorean dress so as not to share his fate.","ideas":["The wise must die for their tenets, not only for country and kin","Flight would make Apollonius a betrayer of his friends","Conscience (to syneidos) accompanies the self everywhere","Damis disguises himself as a layman for the journey to Rome"],"terms":[{"t":"syneidos (conscience)","d":"Inner awareness of one's own deeds which, Apollonius argues, applauds right choices and drives the wrongdoer from temples and society."}]},{"i":1578,"j":1619,"loc":"Book VII 16–22","title":"Arrival in Rome and the meeting with Aelian","summary":"Apollonius reaches Rome, where the praetorian prefect Aelian, who had known him in Egypt, has quietly tried to calm Domitian. Aelian arrests him, silences the accuser, and in a private court reveals his friendship and Domitian's design to use Apollonius against the consulars. He lists the charges: dress, way of life, being worshipped, the Ephesian plague prophecy, hostile sayings, and above all the sacrifice of an Arcadian boy for Nerva. A tribune whom Apollonius once saved at Ephesus mocks him, but Apollonius ignores him; he is sent to the prison of the unbound, telling the anxious Damis that wisdom is not amazed by anything.","ideas":["Aelian protects Apollonius while feigning anger","The full list of charges, centred on the boy-sacrifice","The insolent tribune likened to the voter who ostracized Aristides","Apollonius urges contempt for the despot once known"],"terms":[]},{"i":1620,"j":1665,"loc":"Book VII 23–29","title":"Among the prisoners: consolation and spies","summary":"In prison Apollonius meets men held on absurd charges: a Cilician ruined by his wealth, a Tarentine official who omitted to call Domitian the son of Athena, and an Acarnanian who settled on an Echinades island. Seeing some fifty prisoners despairing, he gives them a discourse on endurance, arguing that life itself is a prison of the soul in the body and that houses, cities and even the earth are prisons. His hearers take heart. A spy sent by Domitian fails to draw him into abuse of the Emperor, and a visitor and then a notary announce that Domitian will see him privately.","ideas":["Wealth that brings anxiety rather than security","Life as a prison: the soul bound in a perishable body","Endurance as a gift of the gods (Archilochus)","Apollonius evades an informer by talking of rivers and animals"],"terms":[{"t":"karteria (endurance)","d":"Patient bearing of adversity, which Apollonius commends to the prisoners as a discovery of the gods."}]},{"i":1666,"j":1707,"loc":"Book VII 30–35","title":"First audience with Domitian; shorn and fettered","summary":"Apollonius tells Damis Phraotes' advice on taming lions, a lesson on handling tyrants, and goes to the palace, comparing it to a bath and rebuking Damis' fear of death. Domitian, sacrificing to Athena, calls him a demon; Apollonius answers that Athena has not yet cleared the Emperor's sight as she did Diomedes'. Asked about Nerva and his friends, he declares them moderate and loyal, enraging Domitian, who has his hair and beard shorn and has him chained among felons. Apollonius retorts that a wizard could not be fettered, and Philostratus rejects a forged Ionic letter claiming he begged for release.","ideas":["Phraotes' advice: neither strike nor flatter the lion","Apollonius defends Nerva, Rufus and Orfitus as innocent","Domitian prejudges the case; Apollonius protests","Philostratus disputes a forged letter of supplication"],"terms":[{"t":"daimōn (demon, spirit)","d":"A superhuman being; Domitian uses the word of Apollonius, who denies it of himself."}]},{"i":1708,"j":1770,"loc":"Book VII 36–42","title":"The Syracusan, the loosed fetter, the Arcadian youth","summary":"A Syracusan agent of Domitian tries to make Apollonius complain or betray his friends; Apollonius parries him and names Euphrates as the informer, later comparing the agent to Python of Byzantium. Asked by Damis if he will be freed, he slips his leg out of the fetters and back, and Damis first grasps his divine nature. Philostratus digresses to denounce magic as a fraud used by athletes, merchants and lovers. Moved to the free prison, Apollonius sends Damis by land to Dicaearchia, promising to appear there, and meets an Arcadian youth jailed for resisting Domitian's advances, whom he commends.","ideas":["Apollonius refuses to lie against his friends to win release","The fetter episode as proof of his superhuman nature","Magic exposed as a money-making deception","The Arcadian youth's chastity under threat of death"],"terms":[{"t":"goēteia (wizardry)","d":"Sorcery for gain, which Philostratus condemns as forbidden by nature and law and distinguishes from Apollonius' wisdom."}]},{"i":1771,"j":1814,"loc":"Book VIII 1–6","title":"The trial before Domitian and the vanishing","summary":"On the way to court Apollonius treats the trial as a dialectical exchange, saying philosophy cares how the Emperor governs. He mocks the order to bring nothing in, and refuses to look at Domitian, gazing instead at the ceiling. Domitian limits the case to four questions: his dress, why men call him a god, the Ephesian plague prophecy, and the sacrifice of the boy, each answered briefly. Applause leads Domitian to acquit him, but when he is told to remain, Apollonius denounces the informers, quotes Homer's line on not being mortal, and vanishes from the court. Philostratus then introduces the prepared speech, defending its elevated style.","ideas":["Four questions and four brief answers","Domitian acquits him but tries to detain him","Apollonius vanishes, leaving no case against his friends","A sage's defence should scorn appeals to pity"],"terms":[]},{"i":1815,"j":1845,"loc":"Book VIII 7.i–iv","title":"The defence speech: Vespasian, poverty, Pythagorean diet","summary":"In the speech he had prepared, Apollonius warns Domitian against unjust hatred of philosophy and against trusting the 'Egyptian sycophant' over the gods. He calls Vespasian as witness: their meetings in Egypt were public, in a temple, before Euphrates and Dion, and concerned laws, wealth and worship, not magic. Wizards, he argues, practise a pseudo-liberal art for gain, whereas he gave away his patrimony, and he reads Vespasian's letter praising his voluntary poverty. He then defends his vegetarian diet as the Pythagorean rule derived from the Brahmans, recounting Pythagoras' memory of having been Euphorbus.","ideas":["Vespasian as witness that Apollonius was no wizard","Wizardry as a deceptive trade for money","Apollonius' renunciation of wealth","The Pythagorean refusal to kill animals for food or dress"],"terms":[{"t":"Pythagorean rule of life","d":"Abstention from animal flesh and animal products, which Apollonius traces from the Brahmans through Egypt to Pythagoras."},{"t":"Euphorbus","d":"The Trojan whom Pythagoras claimed to have been in an earlier life."}]},{"i":1846,"j":1873,"loc":"Book VIII 7.v–ix","title":"Defence speech: dress, hair, divinity, the plague","summary":"Apollonius defends wearing linen as pure, and his long hair as a Dorian and Spartan custom. Against the charge of being called a god, he says he never sought worship; good men have kinship with God and are called divine, as Apollo called Lycurgus, and a wise man may order unruly souls as God orders the universe. On Ephesus he argues that saving a city is no crime, and that foreknowledge came from his light diet, which keeps the senses clear; Socrates, Thales and Anaxagoras foresaw things without being called wizards. He says he captured the plague-demon in the form of an old man and dedicated the deed to Heracles Averter of Evil; the speech breaks off mid-sentence.","ideas":["Linen and uncut hair defended as pure and traditional","Good men are akin to God and so called divine","Foreknowledge arising from a pure diet and clear senses","The plague-demon and the dedication to Heracles"],"terms":[{"t":"Heracles Alexikakos (Averter of Evil)","d":"The god to whom Apollonius credits the stopping of the Ephesian plague, a pure deity unlike the underworld gods of wizards."}]},{"i":1874,"j":1897,"loc":"Book VIII 8–13","title":"Domitian confounded; reappearance at Dicaearchia","summary":"Domitian, instead of hunting Apollonius, is left so bewildered that he loses track of the next case. Before midday Apollonius left the court, and at dusk appears to Demetrius and Damis in the chamber of the nymphs near Dicaearchia, inviting them to touch him to prove he is no ghost. He says he made his defence that very day and attributes the journey to a divine escort; Demetrius recounts Telesinus' dream of Apollonius swimming through fire. Walking to town, he tells them of the trial; Demetrius fears pursuit, Damis affirms his master is divine, and Apollonius sleeps untroubled.","ideas":["The tyrant made a plaything of philosophy","The miraculous same-day journey from Rome","Proof by touch that he is alive in the body","Telesinus' dream as a favourable omen"],"terms":[]},{"i":1898,"j":1940,"loc":"Book VIII 14–23","title":"Return to Hellas: Olympia and Trophonius","summary":"Apollonius sails openly via Sicily to the Peloponnese and stays at Olympia, where crowds from all Greece gather on the rumour he is alive; he makes no boast of his escape. He rebukes an Athenian praising Athena's favour to Domitian, takes a thousand drachmas from Zeus' treasury, and argues with Isagoras that a festival includes its place as well as its people. At Lebadea the priests bar him from the cave of Trophonius, but he descends anyway and emerges after seven days at Aulis with a book of Pythagoras' tenets, later kept at Antium. He warns his followers off forensic orators and, at a solar portent, tells the governor of Hellas that light will follow the night.","ideas":["Greece flocks to Olympia to see the escaped sage","A festival made of its natural place as well as its people","Trophonius endorses Pythagorean philosophy as the purest","Forensic orators blamed for the evils of the prisons"],"terms":[{"t":"Trophonius","d":"An oracular hero at Lebadea whose cave consultants descend into, receiving responses in person."}]},{"i":1941,"j":1972,"loc":"Book VIII 24–31","title":"Domitian's death, Apollonius' passing, and after","summary":"After two years in Greece Apollonius teaches in Ionia. Domitian kills Clemens, and the freedman Stephanus stabs the Emperor, who is finished by his guards; at that moment Apollonius, lecturing in Ephesus, sees the deed and cries out to strike the tyrant, and runners later confirm every detail. Nerva invites him to Rome; Apollonius answers enigmatically and sends Damis away with a letter, so as to die unwitnessed, and Damis' memoirs end there. Philostratus reports varying accounts of his age and end, at Ephesus, Lindus, or in the temple of Dictynna in Crete, and tells how he appeared after death to a doubting youth at Tyana with verses on the soul's immortality.","ideas":["The assassination of Domitian seen at a distance in Ephesus","Live unobserved, and slip unobserved from life","Rival traditions of his death or disappearance","Posthumous teaching that the soul is immortal"],"terms":[{"t":"athanasia psychēs (immortality of the soul)","d":"The doctrine Apollonius affirms in his posthumous verses, while discouraging prying into it during life."}]}]});